WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:08.299
The horseshoe crab...it’s a living fossil
that has called Earth its home for almost

00:00:08.299 --> 00:00:10.059
half a billion years.

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:15.750
It’s outlived dinosaurs and survived mass
extinctions and ice ages, but today it’s

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.180
facing a new threat.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.700
Their adaptations have worked with the way
the Earth has changed and it’s only in recent

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:28.450
years with humans bringing impacts to their
population that they've started to have declines.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:33.130
Rising sea levels, habitat loss and overharvesting
all threaten the population.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:38.660
But if you’ve ever had a vaccine, injection
or a medical implant, then you might not know

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:43.680
that you’ve been relying on this prehistoric
creature’s blood to save your life.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.060
Now, after decades of waiting, a new synthetic
solution could change all of that.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:03.140
Here on our beaches on the Delaware Bay, it
is a place that people are witnessing a phenomena

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.960
that they cannot see anywhere else.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.260
It is the equivalent of the wildebeests crossing
the Serengeti.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.460
In May and June here on the Delaware Bay where
we are right now, millions of crabs come out

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:20.350
on a high tide to lay their eggs about six
inches deep in the sand, and they will stay

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:23.530
in the sand and hatch in about a month.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:29.110
And horseshoe crab eggs are a really critical
part of the ecosystem here.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:35.420
If a single crab is laying almost 100,000
eggs, that is providing a food source for

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:42.409
shorebirds, for gulls, for fish, for terrapins
and then all up the food chain for that.

00:01:42.409 --> 00:01:46.820
And then what happens with the horseshoe crabs
then trickles down to the whole ecosystem here.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.980
They just have managed to evolve with the
changing oceans and the changing land.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.580
The reason this crab has been able to evolve
for so long?

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:58.960
Its blue blood.

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:05.560
This copper-based blood contains special cells
called amebocytes, which are extremely sensitive

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.010
to endotoxins.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:11.220
These are contaminants released from the cell
walls of harmful bacteria and they can cause

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.250
life-threatening fever or toxic shock.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:19.540
As soon as the amebocytes detect any of these
endotoxins, the blood clots around the intruder,

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.239
immobilizing it and protecting the crab from
infection.

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:28.200
In the 1960s scientists found a way to harness
this unique superpower to make sure our medical

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.529
supplies were free from contamination.

00:02:30.529 --> 00:02:33.840
And it replaced slower, more unpredictable
tests involving rabbits.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:42.379
The formula is called limulus amebocyte lysate,
or LAL, and relies on amebocytes taken from

00:02:42.379 --> 00:02:44.689
horseshoe crab blood.

00:02:44.689 --> 00:02:48.699
And so every year half a million crabs are
collected along the Atlantic coast, as well

00:02:48.699 --> 00:02:52.489
as across the eastern shores of Mexico and
China.

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:57.340
A third of the crab’s blood is drawn before
they're released back into the ocean.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:02.439
It’s estimated that 15% of crabs collected
die as a result of this bleeding process,

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.460
which could mean the loss of 75,000 crabs
in the US every year.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.220
An alternative was found, however, almost
20 years ago here in Singapore.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:29.840
In the past say 20 to 30 years ago we managed
to collect 30 pieces in one afternoon, but

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:35.029
now it is difficult to find even a few.

00:03:35.029 --> 00:03:40.609
In the mid-'80s Professor Ding Jeak Ling needed
LAL for work involving IVF embryos, but there

00:03:40.609 --> 00:03:42.079
was a problem.

00:03:42.079 --> 00:03:47.400
Singapore research was not very well funded.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:55.459
So because the LAL was so expensive, we had
to find a good way to understand how the horseshoe

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:58.029
crab blood works.

00:03:58.029 --> 00:04:04.870
So, my research collaborator, also my husband,
together with our research students, went

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:10.640
to the Kranji mudflats to look for horseshoe
crabs, and to bring a few samples back to the lab.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:22.800
We cleaned them, we tagged them, and we took
only a small volume of the blood, isolating

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.950
the blood cells from the horseshoe crab and
we could produce our own equivalent of LAL.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:33.800
This synthetic equivalent is called recombinant
factor C, and it’s a clone of the main gene

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.460
in a horseshoe crab’s blood, which is sensitive
to bacterial endotoxins.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:48.180
It was a moment of realization that it is
going to change the biomedical industry, and

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:54.140
it's going to save a very, very highly threatened
species.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:02.780
But the pharmaceutical companies didn’t
come around as quickly as Professor Ding had hoped.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.840
And so years and then decades passed.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:09.300
We're a highly regulated industry.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.370
And to say we would like to market a new medicine...
a lot of people are reluctant to take a chance

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.680
on trying something new.

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:20.210
That’s until a scientist at pharmaceutical
company Eli Lilly with a particular hobby

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.289
came along.

00:05:21.289 --> 00:05:28.540
Birding is a hobby of mine, so to go to Delaware
Bay and see the horseshoe crab spawning, it

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.900
kind of put it all together for me.

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:36.320
So the horseshoe crab is a keystone species
in its ecology obviously for its own sake

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.680
but then for a lot of other animals that depend
on it.

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:46.210
If we use RFC then there aren't any crabs
that are affected, whether it's mortality

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:51.229
or whether there's some behavioural effect
by taking the blood.

00:05:51.229 --> 00:05:56.879
Studies have shown that the RFC test is a more
effective and a potentially cheaper solution

00:05:56.879 --> 00:05:58.370
than LAL.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:02.270
Changing minds, however, remained the biggest
challenge.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.129
There's been several times I was ready to
throw in the towel.

00:06:06.129 --> 00:06:13.120
So it's been a difficult journey: to fight
internally, to fight externally.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:17.900
Nobody likes change but we think we're doing
it for the right reasons.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:23.159
We have had success and we have the data at
the end of the day.

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:28.930
Persistence paid off, and in 2018, the first
drug to use the recombinant factor C test

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:34.250
was approved by the FDA, and Eli Lilly is
planning to transition 90% of its tests to

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.259
the synthetic by the end of 2020.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:42.160
I think the consequence, if industry carries
on with bleeding crabs, is at some point there

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:43.560
won't be any.

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:48.100
So there are real impacts to what we're doing
and the longer we say, you know, they'll be

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.800
available forever...

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.400
It's likely not true.

00:06:53.409 --> 00:07:00.439
It is important that we as humans are playing
a role in protecting biodiversity and not

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.200
impacting biodiversity.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:07.600
The synthetic version of the horseshoe crab
lysate used by pharmaceutical industry is

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.980
going to have a major impact on horseshoe
crab conservation.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.130
It's not the only factor that we need.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:18.570
We also need to continue with harvest limits
and with beach restoration.

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:25.540
But reducing the need to harvest crabs for
the use of their blood will have a major impact.

